WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.940
[MUSIC]

00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:09.840
If you want to animate a single shape in Game Lab,

00:00:09.984 --> 00:00:13.484
you can quickly end up with a lot of variables to manage.

00:00:13.484 --> 00:00:16.244
You'll need one to store the shape's x position,

00:00:16.244 --> 00:00:19.484
one to store the y position, one for width,

00:00:19.484 --> 00:00:19.968
one for height,

00:00:19.968 --> 00:00:26.968
and as you add fills, strokes, and other characteristics, you'll need even more variables.

00:00:26.968 --> 00:00:29.953
If you want to animate additional shapes, you'll need to

00:00:29.953 --> 00:00:32.793
create a whole other set of variables.

00:00:32.793 --> 00:00:36.113
All this can quickly lead to problems with organization

00:00:36.113 --> 00:00:38.233
and clarity in your code.

00:00:38.233 --> 00:00:39.937
So what's an elegant and manageable

00:00:39.937 --> 00:00:46.077
way to keep track of all these variables? The solution is to start animating with

00:00:46.077 --> 00:00:49.797
sprites. Think of a sprite like a character that

00:00:49.922 --> 00:00:52.202
lives inside your animations.

00:00:52.202 --> 00:00:57.202
At first, a sprite will pretty much just be a rectangle.

00:00:57.342 --> 00:00:59.907
But the beauty of a sprite is what it has

00:00:59.907 --> 00:01:05.067
going on behind the scenes. When you make a sprite, it comes with properties,

00:01:05.067 --> 00:01:09.891
which are variables that store the characteristics of a sprite.

00:01:09.891 --> 00:01:14.131
Properties describe things like where the sprite's located on the screen, what it looks

00:01:14.131 --> 00:01:17.451
like, and what color it is.

00:01:17.451 --> 00:01:19.851
To create a new sprite, use the createSprite

00:01:19.876 --> 00:01:24.516
command. You can assign your sprite a label just like you would a variable.

00:01:25.876 --> 00:01:29.860
This code is creating a new sprite and giving it the label mySprite,

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:34.540
but you can label it anything you'd like.

00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:38.380
Just like shapes, sprites need to be drawn to the screen.

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:39.845
Use the drawSprites command

00:01:39.845 --> 00:01:42.445
to see your sprite appear in the display area.
